Identify the full name and historical context of James Douglas, 3rd Earl of Angus to determine the correct individual and lineage.
James Douglas, 3rd Earl of Angus, was a Scottish nobleman born in 1426 and died in 1446. He was the son of William Douglas, 2nd Earl of Angus, and Margaret Hay of Yester. He succeeded to the title at the age of eleven and was involved in conflicts with his Black Douglas cousins. His estates were forfeited by King James II in 1445 under the influence of his near cousin William Douglas, 8th Earl of Douglas. He was briefly betrothed to Princess Joan, the third daughter of King James I, and was succeeded by his brother, George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus, in 1446. The Douglas family held significant power in Scotland, and their lineage continued through various titles such as Marquess of Douglas and Earl of Angus.
