The identified mother in question is Margaret Beaufort, the mother of Henry VII, and her historical accuracy as a claimant to the English throne can be verified through multiple reliable sources on English medieval royalty. Margaret Beaufort was the daughter of John Beaufort, 1st Earl of Somerset, and Margaret Beauchamp. John Beaufort was the son of John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster, and thus a grandson of Edward III. Although John Beaufort was born out of wedlock, he was legitimized by royal decree in 1404, which allowed his descendants to inherit titles and lands.

Despite this legitimization, there was a condition that the Beaufort line forfeited their rights to the crown. However, Margaret Beaufort's claim to the throne was strengthened through her lineage, as she was a descendant of Edward III via her father, John Beaufort. This made her a legitimate heir to the Plantagenet dynasty, which was crucial in her son Henry VII's claim to the throne after the Wars of the Roses.

Her role in securing the Tudor succession is well-documented in historical records and scholarly works, including accounts from the time period and subsequent historical analyses. Therefore, the historical accuracy of Margaret Beaufort as the mother of Henry VII and her claim to the English throne is supported by multiple reliable sources.
