Identify the full name and historical context of Thomas Lyon-Bowes, Lord Glamis to determine his familial lineage and potential ancestral origins.
Thomas Lyon-Bowes, Lord Glamis, was born on 6 February 1801 and died on 27 January 1834. He was the only son of Thomas Lyon-Bowes, 11th Earl of Strathmore and Kinghorne, and Mary Elizabeth Louisa Rodney Carpenter. As the eldest son, he became the heir to the Earldom of Strathmore and Kinghorne. Thomas Lyon-Bowes, Lord Glamis, is the great grandfather of Queen Elizabeth, the Queen Mother. He married Charlotte Grimstead on 21 December 1820, who was the daughter of Joseph Valentine Grimstead of Ewood Park.

His familial lineage traces back to the Lyon-Bowes family, which is part of the broader Lyons family. The Lyons family is an eminent noble Anglo-Norman family descended from Ingelram de Lyons, Lord of Lyons, who arrived in England with the Norman Conquest. The family originated in the district of the Forest of Lyons, north of the town of Lyons-la-Forêt in Haute Normandie, where the family seat was the Castle of Lyons. The original surname was 'de Lyons' ('of Lyons').
